WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.298 --> 00:00:02.570
- [Marchers] Macy's, Macy's Day Parade.

00:00:02.570 --> 00:00:05.580
- [Narrator] Macy's is
the brand long identified

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:08.650
with the kickoff of the
holiday shopping season.

00:00:08.650 --> 00:00:12.770
And for years, it spent big
to identify its star logo

00:00:12.770 --> 00:00:14.170
with star power.

00:00:14.170 --> 00:00:17.740
- Almost midnight!
- Macy's black Friday sale.

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:20.970
- [Narrator] But lately it
hasn't been all holiday cheer.

00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:23.380
And that was before the coronavirus crisis

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:25.970
forced it to temporarily
shutter its doors.

00:00:25.970 --> 00:00:28.920
The retailer has fared better
than some of its peers.

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:32.750
In the first half of 2020,
18 major retailers have filed

00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:34.520
for Chapter 11 protection.

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:38.150
Macy's has not, but the
historic company is hurting,

00:00:38.150 --> 00:00:41.823
raising questions about how
far it's star could fall.

00:00:43.810 --> 00:00:47.610
Macy's first opened as a dry
goods store on 14th street

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:50.340
in Manhattan in 1858.

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:51.470
Near the turn of the century,

00:00:51.470 --> 00:00:53.370
two German Jewish immigrant brothers,

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:56.310
Nathan and Isidor Straus
bought a controlling interest

00:00:56.310 --> 00:00:57.630
in the company.

00:00:57.630 --> 00:01:00.660
And in 1902, they moved it 20 blocks north

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:04.330
to the store's present flagship
location at Herald Square.

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:07.430
It became part of an upscale
New York shopping district

00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:09.700
known as Ladies' Mile.

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:12.150
The brothers took the
company public in 1911

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:14.140
on the New York Stock Exchange.

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:16.480
The following year, Isidor Straus died,

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:19.850
along with his wife, Ida,
in the Titanic disaster.

00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:22.020
Nathan Strauss continued
growing the business.

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:25.480
And in 1924, the company
expanded to take over

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:29.070
nearly the entire block
across from Herald Square.

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:32.180
And the retailer heralded
itself as the largest store

00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:33.690
in the world.

00:01:33.690 --> 00:01:35.810
That same year, 10,000 people gathered

00:01:35.810 --> 00:01:38.240
in front of his store to
watch a small procession,

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:39.720
which would eventually become

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:42.440
the Macy's Thanksgiving Day Parade.

00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:44.640
The company survived the Great Depression.

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:47.540
And by the mid 1940s,
with American servicemen

00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:51.090
returning home from World
War II, business was booming.

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:53.820
Single-day holiday sales
past a million dollars

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:56.300
for two years straight,
which would be more than

00:01:56.300 --> 00:01:58.590
14 million dollars today.

00:01:58.590 --> 00:01:59.660
During the Cold War,

00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:02.360
the brand became a symbol
of American capitalism

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:04.170
and consumer choice.

00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:06.500
The store was shown off
to a top communist leader

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:09.630
during his 1959 diplomatic
visit to the US.

00:02:09.630 --> 00:02:11.201
- [Reporter] Before the store opens,

00:02:11.201 --> 00:02:13.390
(indistinct) is escorted through Macy's

00:02:13.390 --> 00:02:16.720
for a cross section of what's
offered the American shopper.

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:18.650
- [Narrator] The company
expanded around the country.

00:02:18.650 --> 00:02:22.400
By the mid 1970s, it had a
chain of 72 department stores

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:23.580
in 10 States.

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:25.800
Sales grew into the 1980s.

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:29.280
Then in 1986, the
retailer's Chairman and CEO,

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:32.250
Edward Finkelstein took
the company private

00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:35.803
in a 3.5 billion dollar
leveraged buyout deal.

00:02:37.130 --> 00:02:40.370
- And the leverage buyout
loaded the company with debt

00:02:40.370 --> 00:02:42.210
and that debt eventually tipped it

00:02:42.210 --> 00:02:44.675
into bankruptcy protection.

00:02:44.675 --> 00:02:47.080
- [Narrator] In 1994, Federated, a rival

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:49.040
department store chain based in Ohio,

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:51.010
agreed to acquire the brand.

00:02:51.010 --> 00:02:53.880
- They bought Macy's out of bankruptcy.

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:58.430
And then you fast forward to 2005

00:02:58.430 --> 00:03:00.770
when Federated at the
time was the number one

00:03:00.770 --> 00:03:02.570
department store chain in the country,

00:03:02.570 --> 00:03:05.100
it merged with the number-two player,

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:07.100
May Department Stores Company.

00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:10.240
And renamed itself Macy's,
and that's where we get

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:13.450
the sort of current
incarnation of Macy's, Inc.

00:03:13.450 --> 00:03:15.640
- [Narrator] It was a
landmark deal that created

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:18.330
the first truly national
department store chain

00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:19.503
with stores from coast.

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:23.270
- And they had beloved brands,

00:03:23.270 --> 00:03:26.490
Marshall Fields in Chicago, Filene's.

00:03:26.490 --> 00:03:29.670
They had sort of these local
brands around the country

00:03:29.670 --> 00:03:31.970
that they eventually did
away with and renamed

00:03:31.970 --> 00:03:33.600
all the stories Macy's.

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:34.800
- [Narrator] Federated
sought to give all of them

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:37.860
a coherent image with
splashy national TV ads

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:39.390
that starred the celebrities

00:03:39.390 --> 00:03:42.050
who sold branded products in their stores.

00:03:42.050 --> 00:03:44.680
- [Spokesperson] Only one
star can bring all these stars

00:03:44.680 --> 00:03:47.330
together, that's the magic of Macy's.

00:03:47.330 --> 00:03:50.030
- It's good to be back here at Macy's.

00:03:50.030 --> 00:03:51.380
Thank you all for coming.

00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:54.140
I'm very excited for my new shoe line.

00:03:54.140 --> 00:03:56.000
- [Narrator] And then the economy tumbled.

00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:58.370
- [Reporter] Signs of a
recession we're in the air,

00:03:58.370 --> 00:04:00.130
between dismal retail figures

00:04:00.130 --> 00:04:01.840
and the subprime mortgage crisis.

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:03.720
- [Narrator] In 2008, the Great Recession

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:06.640
brought a sharp slowdown
in consumer spending.

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:09.233
And steep losses to the
company's balance sheet.

00:04:09.233 --> 00:04:12.990
- The Great Recession of 2008,
was challenging for all the

00:04:12.990 --> 00:04:17.551
brick and mortar chains,
and Macy's in particular

00:04:17.551 --> 00:04:19.770
struggled during that period.

00:04:19.770 --> 00:04:21.510
- [Narrator] It wasn't until 2010,

00:04:21.510 --> 00:04:23.760
that Macy's returned to profitability,

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:28.230
thanks in part to double-digit
growth in online sales.

00:04:28.230 --> 00:04:29.650
By this point, it operated more than

00:04:29.650 --> 00:04:32.060
800 department stores in the US.

00:04:32.060 --> 00:04:35.270
- [Reporter] For the first
time, all 800 Macy's stores

00:04:35.270 --> 00:04:37.570
will open at midnight on Thanksgiving.

00:04:37.570 --> 00:04:39.650
- [Narrator] But for the
retailer and its peers,

00:04:39.650 --> 00:04:42.870
the post-recession good
times didn't last long.

00:04:42.870 --> 00:04:47.420
- All of a sudden, the impact
of Amazon and online shopping

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:50.940
really started to eat into
their sales and margins,

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:53.930
and traditional brick
and mortar chains woke up

00:04:53.930 --> 00:04:56.920
to the reality that they
had way too many stores.

00:04:56.920 --> 00:05:00.530
And chains like Macy's, The Gap,

00:05:00.530 --> 00:05:02.400
these beloved sort of retail brands

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:06.230
that were in every mall
started closing stores.

00:05:06.230 --> 00:05:09.330
- Big box retailer Macy's is
saying goodbye to three malls

00:05:09.330 --> 00:05:10.780
right here in Hampton Roads,

00:05:10.780 --> 00:05:14.220
leaving a big impact on
more than 250 employees.

00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:17.060
- And a big heartbreak
to thousands of shoppers.

00:05:17.060 --> 00:05:18.080
- [Narrator] The company continued

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:20.010
cutting costs, closing stores.

00:05:20.010 --> 00:05:23.970
And in 2014, it laid off
1800 workers to try to save

00:05:23.970 --> 00:05:26.470
a hundred million dollars a year.

00:05:26.470 --> 00:05:29.520
The good news for Macy's,
it had an online strategy

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:30.830
that was working.

00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:33.770
Macy's.com became the seventh most popular

00:05:33.770 --> 00:05:35.810
online store in the US.

00:05:35.810 --> 00:05:39.440
But online sales proved
to be less profitable.

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:42.530
- For traditional retailers like Macy's,

00:05:42.530 --> 00:05:44.070
shifting your business online,

00:05:44.070 --> 00:05:45.710
presents particular challenges.

00:05:45.710 --> 00:05:48.860
Online sales tend to be
less profitable than sales

00:05:48.860 --> 00:05:49.820
in a physical store.

00:05:49.820 --> 00:05:51.310
You've got all kinds of,

00:05:51.310 --> 00:05:54.480
shipping costs really eat
into the profit margin.

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:56.310
- [Narrator] Then came the pandemic.

00:05:56.310 --> 00:05:57.350
- [Reporter] Businesses across country

00:05:57.350 --> 00:05:58.420
forced to shut their doors,

00:05:58.420 --> 00:06:01.390
already leading to
absolutely stunning increases

00:06:01.390 --> 00:06:02.550
in unemployment claims.

00:06:02.550 --> 00:06:05.690
- All that inventory
literally got locked inside.

00:06:05.690 --> 00:06:09.080
And now it's become an
aging asset inside stores

00:06:09.080 --> 00:06:10.286
across the country.

00:06:10.286 --> 00:06:13.570
- The country's shut down and
people aren't buying stuff.

00:06:13.570 --> 00:06:15.000
- [Narrator] After months-long closures,

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:16.820
beginning in March of 2020,

00:06:16.820 --> 00:06:20.150
Macy's posted over 900
million dollars in losses

00:06:20.150 --> 00:06:22.760
for the first three quarters of 2020.

00:06:22.760 --> 00:06:25.570
Despite this, it was in a
better position to survive

00:06:25.570 --> 00:06:27.050
than many of its peers.

00:06:27.050 --> 00:06:29.430
- Well, the lessons they learned
from their leveraged buyout

00:06:29.430 --> 00:06:31.510
in the '80s really stayed with them.

00:06:31.510 --> 00:06:34.540
And for a retailer to
take on too much debt

00:06:34.540 --> 00:06:35.510
is very perilous.

00:06:35.510 --> 00:06:38.030
This is what has pushed
a lot of companies,

00:06:38.030 --> 00:06:41.260
from JC Penny, to Neiman Marcus,
to J.Crew into bankruptcy.

00:06:41.260 --> 00:06:43.910
- [Narrator] To avoid debt,
the retailer already had plans

00:06:43.910 --> 00:06:47.040
before the pandemic to
close a fifth of its stores

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:48.780
over the next three years.

00:06:48.780 --> 00:06:51.810
And on top of that, in June,
it said it planned to lay off

00:06:51.810 --> 00:06:54.850
nearly 4,000 corporate staffers.

00:06:54.850 --> 00:06:57.430
A Macy spokesperson says
that by reorganizing

00:06:57.430 --> 00:07:00.200
how the company's structured
and reducing costs,

00:07:00.200 --> 00:07:02.540
it's in a good position
to win with customers

00:07:02.540 --> 00:07:04.650
and emerge a stronger company.

00:07:04.650 --> 00:07:06.390
- Not all the stores look great.

00:07:06.390 --> 00:07:08.770
I mean, they've got
maybe half their fleet,

00:07:08.770 --> 00:07:10.870
they've been investing in and upgrading,

00:07:10.870 --> 00:07:14.650
and making into kind of marquee locations.

00:07:14.650 --> 00:07:18.260
Macy's has to find a reason to exist.

00:07:18.260 --> 00:07:21.130
Why should customers come to
its stores when they can get

00:07:21.130 --> 00:07:23.950
so many of the products
it sells elsewhere?

00:07:23.950 --> 00:07:26.450
And sometimes for lower prices.

00:07:26.450 --> 00:07:28.340
- [Narrator] One positive for Macy's,

00:07:28.340 --> 00:07:31.040
as its competitors fold,
the company has a chance

00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:32.510
to pick up market share.

00:07:32.510 --> 00:07:35.230
Most stores have reopened,
and it's still got

00:07:35.230 --> 00:07:37.110
the annual marketing bonanza

00:07:37.110 --> 00:07:40.360
that is the Macy's
Thanksgiving Day Parade.

00:07:40.360 --> 00:07:44.510
Though, in 2020, it will
be a television-only event

00:07:44.510 --> 00:07:46.963
without the in-person crowds.

